A live Hysteria2 stand: our client against the REFERENCE server, on one machine, with nothing
production anywhere near it.

The current local qualification driver is qualify.py; see
../../docs/HYSTERIA-COMPATIBILITY.md for the pinned 2.13.0 server preparation,
loopback mixed-load/obfuscation matrix, negative control and standalone evidence
replay. Its unit tests run in make check. The older Docker orchestration below
retains its original process names; use a clean, isolated stand when replaying
it. Reference executables are rebuilt from the current vendor pin on every run.

WHY IT EXISTS. The uplink path in lc-tunnel — the read from the client and what happens to those
bytes before hy_pump hands them to the QUIC stream — is a seam, and no unit suite in this tree can
see it: the pieces are all correct on their own, and `make check` proves the program COMPILES with
the plane and that every format it parses is the reference's. Whether the bytes arrive is a
different question, and it needs a node. This makes one out of vendor/.

  vendor/hysteria/app   built with the Go in lc-build:local, run as `server`
  echo/                 an echo target on the SERVER's own loopback, so the only path the bytes
                        travel that belongs to us is the tunnel
  load/                 pushes a position-dependent pattern up and checks every byte that returns

The pattern is a function of the offset, so a truncation, a duplicated chunk or a reordering shows
as a mismatch at a named position instead of as a length that happens to agree.

RUN IT:

    sh test/hystand/run.sh

About two and a half minutes, two containers on the `lcnet` docker network, both removed at the end.

After the bytes, the connection's life, which is the reference's reconnectableClientImpl
(vendor/hysteria/core/client/reconnect.go): the node is paused, and the client must end the
connection on its idle timeout, dial nothing while nobody asks, refuse the first request after that
at once and without dialling — the reference still holds the dead client until a call fails on it —
make exactly one attempt for the next, failed by the handshake's five seconds of silence, and make
the connection itself for the first request after the node is back.

Then the node's hysteria is killed with its container still up, so every packet to the port is
answered with ICMP port unreachable: the connection must live through twenty seconds of that and end
only on its idle timeout, because the reference's socket is unconnected and never hears of ICMP. And
last, a client started while the node is paused must exit with status 1 after the handshake's
silence, never having listened — the reference's "failed to initialize client".

⚠️ AND RUN THE CONTROL, which is the half that gets skipped. A stand that cannot fail reports
nothing, and this one is easy to leave testing the loopback instead of the tunnel. Break the client
on purpose and watch it fail — from a copy of the file, not `git checkout`, which also takes any
uncommitted work with it.

The uplink is read by the reader thread (src/net/reader.c), which reads as much as the queue has
room for and adds what it got to the queue's length under the stage lock. Lose a byte of every read:

    cp src/net/reader.c /tmp/reader.c.orig
    sed -i 's|        \*st->len += (size_t)got;|        *st->len += (size_t)got - 1u;|' src/net/reader.c
    sh test/hystand/run.sh          # must print MISMATCH (2026-09-23: at byte 18692)
    cp /tmp/reader.c.orig src/net/reader.c

It replaced the two controls written for the loop's own read (`HY_READ_ROUNDS` in
src/cmd/lc-tunnel/main.c), which the reader thread made dead: their sed matched nothing, the stand
passed, and a control that changes nothing looks exactly like one that was run.

⚠️ AND THE CONTROL THAT IS NOT HERE, on purpose. The reader stops when the queue is full, and the
loop wakes it once it has made room (lc_reader_rearm). This stand sees neither half. With the
reader's own compaction removed every scenario still passes, because the loop compacts the same
queue under the same lock (hy_feed_up); with the loop's re-arm after hy_feed_up taken out they
pass too, only slower — 891 Mbit/s round trip against 1166, and 3378 write passes with nothing to
write against 491 — because the loop re-arms from a second place as well. Both were tried on
2026-09-23 as the second control and both came back green. That path is held by test/reader
instead, where nothing else re-arms: a queue of sixteen bytes, filled, drained and re-armed, with
the no-op re-arm caught as a mutant.

For the lifecycle, four controls, each put in and taken out the same way:

    reconnect while idle: `if (hy.on && !hy.up && hy.pending == 0) (void)hy_connect(loop);` before
                          the loop's `sessions_expire(loop);`        — must FAIL "connect attempts
                          in 20 s with no requests"
    refuse while down:    `if (!hy.up) return -1;` at the top of hy_start — must FAIL "a request
                          while the node is silent": no attempt, refused at once
    connected socket:     `(void)connect(fd, (struct sockaddr *)out_addr, *out_len);` after
                          `c->peer_addr = *out_addr;` in dial_node (src/quic/conn.c) — must FAIL,
                          and on this stand it does so at start: the client's first Initial
                          reaches the server's port before hysteria has bound it, and the
                          connected socket takes that ICMP as "cannot send: Connection refused"
                          where the reference simply sends the Initial again
    stay up at start:     drop the `return LC_EXIT_FAILED;` after "failed to initialize client"
                          in main — must FAIL the last check, with exit 124 from its timeout

NOT PART OF `make check`, deliberately. The gate runs with `--network none` and builds from the tree
alone; building the reference server needs the Go module cache, which is a volume on the machine
that has already built it once. Wiring this in would make the gate depend on a download, and a gate
that fails for a reason that is not the code is a gate people learn to ignore. It is a tool, like
test/storm, and it is named in the commit that needed it.
